WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.590
- My next guest shot to fame

00:00:01.590 --> 00:00:03.807
after she tweeted a
video of herself singing

00:00:03.807 --> 00:00:05.900
"At Last" by Etta James.

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:06.850
Great song.

00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:10.320
That tweet was retweeted by
none other than Justin Bieber.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:11.153
Who?

00:00:11.153 --> 00:00:12.140
Never heard of him.

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:13.126
As they say,
(audience laughs)

00:00:13.126 --> 00:00:15.330
(laughs) the rest is history.

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:18.130
She's now set to drop
her debut studio album.

00:00:18.130 --> 00:00:19.470
It's called "Life Support",

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:21.790
and her single "Baby" is out right now.

00:00:21.790 --> 00:00:23.944
Please welcome Madison Beer.

00:00:23.944 --> 00:00:28.944
(audience claps and cheers)
(upbeat music)

00:00:32.144 --> 00:00:34.061
You're adorable, hello!

00:00:35.118 --> 00:00:36.544
Oh my gosh, you look adorable.

00:00:36.544 --> 00:00:37.377
- Oh my god, so you do.

00:00:37.377 --> 00:00:38.210
- I love your outfit.

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:39.043
Nice to meet you.

00:00:39.043 --> 00:00:40.460
- You too, I'm such a big fan.

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:42.100
- Oh thank you, thank you so much.

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:44.380
I was literally watching your
stuff right before the show.

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:45.213
I was like, wait,
- Really?

00:00:45.213 --> 00:00:46.046
- Oh my god, thank you.
I want to hear all the stuff.

00:00:46.046 --> 00:00:46.879
I'm excited for you.

00:00:46.879 --> 00:00:47.807
It's "Baby" right?

00:00:47.807 --> 00:00:48.640
It's the new one right?
- Yeah,

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:49.473
"Baby" just came out, yeah.

00:00:49.473 --> 00:00:51.020
Thank you, thank you so much.
- Yeah, it's so exciting.

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:52.590
I still, I know I've
been in it for a minute,

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:54.410
but I still remember when
my first song came out.

00:00:54.410 --> 00:00:55.823
Like it was just so exciting,

00:00:55.823 --> 00:00:57.020
like the first like big one
- Yeah.

00:00:57.020 --> 00:00:58.690
- That I was like, "Man,
people are listening

00:00:58.690 --> 00:00:59.523
to this on the radio,

00:00:59.523 --> 00:01:00.356
it's so cool."
- It's really exciting,

00:01:00.356 --> 00:01:01.189
yeah.
- Yeah.

00:01:01.189 --> 00:01:03.590
Well tell me about your
debut album and new single.

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:06.190
- Yeah, my album is coming
out hopefully really soon.

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:07.970
We're just kind of
doing the final touches,

00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:09.880
and it's my first album,
so I want it to obviously

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:11.177
be perfect,
- Yeah.

00:01:11.177 --> 00:01:13.280
- and yeah, it's just, you know,

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:16.010
it's finally I feel like
my way of telling my story

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:17.610
and really telling a
narrative that I want to,

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:18.900
and I feel like I'm touching on things

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:20.700
that I haven't yet been public about

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:23.050
or spoken about, and it's
just like, it's my story,

00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:23.940
and so I'm just trying to make it perfect,

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:25.480
- Yeah.
- and I love it so much,

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:26.770
and it's like my child.

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:29.080
I was gonna say it's my
baby, but "Baby" is my baby.

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:30.502
So that's my recent single.
- Yes, that's amazing.

00:01:30.502 --> 00:01:31.990
- And yeah, it's great.

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:33.540
That song was honestly
like I thought of one

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:36.110
of the perfect, we've
had two singles prior

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:37.610
to "Baby" come out off of the album,

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:40.430
but I felt like it was the
perfect one to release right now,

00:01:40.430 --> 00:01:42.470
because I wanted to give people confidence

00:01:42.470 --> 00:01:43.303
and make people feel good,
- Yeah.

00:01:43.303 --> 00:01:44.750
- and I think there's something to be said

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:46.090
of like, when you're doing your makeup,

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:47.217
and you go to put a song
on, you want something

00:01:47.217 --> 00:01:48.743
that's gonna make you feel good,

00:01:48.743 --> 00:01:49.576
- Yeah.
- and something

00:01:49.576 --> 00:01:51.187
that's gonna boost your
confidence, and this is that.

00:01:51.187 --> 00:01:52.020
- Something that makes you feel sexy.

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:52.853
Yeah.
- Yeah, I wanted something

00:01:52.853 --> 00:01:53.686
that people were just gonna be like,

00:01:53.686 --> 00:01:54.860
"This makes me feel hot."

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:55.820
Period, that's it.
- Yeah.

00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:56.653
- And I just really like--

00:01:56.653 --> 00:01:57.486
- Nothing wrong

00:01:57.486 --> 00:01:58.319
with that feeling (laughs).
- Yes.

00:01:58.319 --> 00:01:59.152
So that's what I wanted to do.

00:01:59.152 --> 00:02:00.680
And so "Baby" is doing that,

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:02.827
and I get tweets every day
from people being like,

00:02:02.827 --> 00:02:04.590
"This song makes me
feel good about myself,"

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:05.910
and that's all I could ever ask for.

00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:08.370
- So you've been open about
your mental health struggles,

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:11.140
which is amazing as an
artist to be that vulnerable,

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:12.770
because those are the ones I feel like

00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:13.690
that have longevity,
- Yeah.

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:16.190
- because you can trust
them, and it's believable.

00:02:17.325 --> 00:02:18.230
And I love that as well,

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:20.360
because you probably have a lot of fans

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:22.130
that look up to you and maybe
they have the same struggles,

00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:24.010
so it's very cool to kind
of be open about that.

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:25.970
But how does it feel, 'cause I know

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:27.690
that a lot of things have
happened to me publicly,

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:29.270
- Yeah (laughs).
- or currently publicly,

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:31.317
and it's hard to go through.
- It is.

00:02:31.317 --> 00:02:33.780
- And how does it, does it empower you,

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:36.150
or is it scary, or is it both?

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:37.730
- Well yeah, I think
it's definitely a balance

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:39.500
of both for me, particularly I decided

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:40.940
I wanted to start being open about it,

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:42.770
because one of the main
things when I was really

00:02:42.770 --> 00:02:45.590
in the thick of like
being massively depressed

00:02:45.590 --> 00:02:46.970
and not even have the motivation to like

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:48.140
get out of bed in the morning,
- Yeah.

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:49.990
- I felt so isolated and so alone,

00:02:49.990 --> 00:02:52.210
because especially in the
day and age of social media,

00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:53.440
and we're all guilty of it,

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:55.150
everyone's life looks so perfect,

00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:56.510
and everyone seems so
- It's filtered.

00:02:56.510 --> 00:02:58.570
- abundantly happy that
when you're miserable,

00:02:58.570 --> 00:02:59.460
you're like, "Why?"

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:01.450
This, you feel so horrible about yourself.

00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:03.570
And so I'm trying to
make it clear to people

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:06.149
that what you see is what
we choose to show you.

00:03:06.149 --> 00:03:07.090
- It's (indistinct), yeah.
- I'm choosing,

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:09.430
I'm completely curating what I'm posting

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:11.410
on my Instagram, I'm
curating what I'm tweeting,

00:03:11.410 --> 00:03:13.360
and I can totally make myself seem

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:15.060
like a 100% happy person.
- Yeah.

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:16.250
- So on the days that I feel like

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:19.040
I see something that might
be a quote that's inspiring

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:20.370
or something that might
help somebody else,

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:22.790
I post it, 'cause I'm like
I hope someone sees this

00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:23.860
who's not as happy
- Yeah.

00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:24.693
- as I am today maybe.

00:03:24.693 --> 00:03:27.020
- I read a book recently, and it was like,

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:29.250
am I seeing your
representative or like you?

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:30.240
- Right, exactly (laughs).
- Well some days,

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:32.402
you see my representative.
- Yeah, no for real.

00:03:32.402 --> 00:03:34.299
- (laughs) 'Cause some days,
you don't want to see me,

00:03:34.299 --> 00:03:35.527
(laughs) I'm like yeah.
- Exactly, me too,

00:03:35.527 --> 00:03:36.360
I feel you girl.

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:38.160
- So I love that you're
so open about that.

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:41.410
It's very, very important
for us as just as a society.

00:03:41.410 --> 00:03:42.720
Speaking of emotional support,

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:44.750
a lot of people online want to know

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:47.628
if you sleep with
stuffed animals (laughs).

00:03:47.628 --> 00:03:48.540
- I do sleep
(Kelly laughs)

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:49.867
with stuffed animals,

00:03:49.867 --> 00:03:51.328
and I'm saying that on television.

00:03:51.328 --> 00:03:52.640
I do, I do.
- I love it.

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:55.530
Is it like a specific one from childhood?

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:57.630
- From my whole life, I've had a unicorn

00:03:57.630 --> 00:03:58.720
and a rabbit,
- Oh my god.

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:01.010
- little stuffed animals
on my bed forever.

00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:01.843
And I always tell my friends,

00:04:01.843 --> 00:04:03.390
I'm like, "If my house is burning down,

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:04.870
that is what you get."
- That's what I'm grabbing.

00:04:04.870 --> 00:04:05.703
- Don't care

00:04:05.703 --> 00:04:06.536
about anything else.
(Kelly laughs)

00:04:06.536 --> 00:04:07.600
You get the stuffed
animals, and that's it.

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:08.433
Like I guard them.

00:04:08.433 --> 00:04:09.700
I just moved into a house recently.

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:11.450
- Yeah.
- And they were, obviously,

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:13.500
like I had movers there,
and I had my stuffed animals

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:14.687
with me in my bag, and I was like,

00:04:14.687 --> 00:04:15.950
"These aren't getting lost in the move."

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:16.783
Because you know whenever you move,

00:04:16.783 --> 00:04:17.616
it's like something
- Yeah.

00:04:17.616 --> 00:04:18.710
- gets lost in the move.

00:04:18.710 --> 00:04:20.797
I would, you lose me instead.

00:04:20.797 --> 00:04:21.630
Lose me.
(Kelly laughs)

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:22.870
Don't lose the stuffed animals.

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:23.703
Like they are my lifeline.
- Yeah.

00:04:23.703 --> 00:04:25.170
I love that you have something like that,

00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:26.150
it's such a comfort thing,
- It is.

00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:28.420
- especially for people
that travel a lot, you know?

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:30.100
Like, well I guess we don't
travel as much anymore,

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:31.440
but we still do travel a bit

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:33.440
more than the average person
if you're in the industry.

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:35.930
And it's nice to have just
whatever your comfort is.

00:04:35.930 --> 00:04:37.021
- It's literally there,

00:04:37.021 --> 00:04:37.854
the loves of my life.
- I just love

00:04:37.854 --> 00:04:38.780
that it's cute stuffed animals.

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:39.626
- They're the loves of my life.

00:04:39.626 --> 00:04:40.800
- It's really my favorite
story of the day (laughs).

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:41.760
- Yeah, they really, really are.

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:43.690
Like, Allison, they have names.

00:04:43.690 --> 00:04:45.440
- Allison the unicorn
- Oh my god, Allison (laughs).

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:47.500
- Allison the unicorn, she
used to be bright pink.

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:49.070
Now she's like the palest pink

00:04:49.070 --> 00:04:50.130
you could ever imagine.
(Kelly laughs)

00:04:50.130 --> 00:04:51.030
I've had her my whole life,

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:52.236
but it's just, it's again,

00:04:52.236 --> 00:04:53.069
this goes into the--
- Wait,

00:04:53.069 --> 00:04:53.902
what's your rabbit's name?

00:04:53.902 --> 00:04:55.930
- His name is very creatively Bunny.

00:04:55.930 --> 00:04:57.456
That's his name, yes,
- (laughs) Very creative.

00:04:57.456 --> 00:04:58.780
It's very unique.
- it's a very creative name.

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:00.340
But this is also like another thing

00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:02.660
that I believe is like a
healthy coping mechanism

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:03.493
that I've come up with.

00:05:03.493 --> 00:05:04.430
I'm like that's another,
- Yeah

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:06.040
- it goes into the mental
health conversation,

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:06.873
'cause these are things

00:05:06.873 --> 00:05:08.050
that when I'm having an anxiety attack,

00:05:08.050 --> 00:05:09.400
they supply me with comfort,
- Yeah.

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:10.490
- and they make me feel better.

00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:11.870
Whatever that placebo might be,

00:05:11.870 --> 00:05:13.160
- Yeah.
- it does make me feel better,

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:14.257
so my friends

00:05:14.257 --> 00:05:15.090
all know about it.
- It is interesting,

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:16.700
social media and the
correlation of like anxiety,

00:05:16.700 --> 00:05:19.670
because I know a lot of
girls around your age

00:05:19.670 --> 00:05:22.190
that do suffer from
anxiety or panic attacks,

00:05:22.190 --> 00:05:24.470
and a lot of it I think does
kind of come back around

00:05:24.470 --> 00:05:26.620
to how negative sometimes,

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:28.490
it can be a beautiful
thing and very helpful,

00:05:28.490 --> 00:05:29.323
social media,
- Right, there's (indistinct).

00:05:29.323 --> 00:05:31.260
- but it can be negative as well if you,

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:33.273
don't watch The Social
Dilemma, unless you're ready.

00:05:33.273 --> 00:05:34.106
- I've seen it.
- Oh my gosh.

00:05:34.106 --> 00:05:35.900
- Trust me, I live by the social,

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:37.930
I love that documentary.
- I was like,

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:40.184
I don't want to be this guy.

00:05:40.184 --> 00:05:41.331
I was like (laughs).
- No, right, right,

00:05:41.331 --> 00:05:42.650
that's the creepy thing
- Yes, so I was like, uh,

00:05:42.650 --> 00:05:43.747
when they have the dummy like this.

00:05:43.747 --> 00:05:44.990
I'm like, I don't want to do that.

00:05:44.990 --> 00:05:45.950
- new goal in life,

00:05:45.950 --> 00:05:47.630
to not be that dude.
- Not ever be that.

00:05:47.630 --> 00:05:49.410
- Yes, it was such an interesting thing.

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:51.460
But there's positive and
negatives of everything, but,

00:05:51.460 --> 00:05:52.293
- Totally.
- well everybody,

00:05:52.293 --> 00:05:53.920
Madison's single is called "Baby".

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:55.300
You can listen to it now.
(audience claps and cheers)

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:58.290
And look out for her debut album
"Life Support" coming soon.

00:05:58.290 --> 00:06:00.343
Cool title by the way.
- Thank you.

